WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.147
Pedal Post's depot has two doors.

2
00:00:05.147 --> 00:00:13.925
Riders who work there every day come in at the front, and speak the house shorthand: PP-7781, collected, two and a half.

3
00:00:14.067 --> 00:00:20.250
Drivers from a partner courier use the side door, where a clerk hands them the standard printed form.

4
00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:24.107
Both doors lead to the same sorting room, under the same rules.

5
00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:26.189
Our library needs both doors.

6
00:00:26.189 --> 00:00:28.642
The front door is for Kotlin callers.

7
00:00:28.642 --> 00:00:32.223
The side door is for the support team, who write Java.

8
00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:36.224
Now, a question that's easy to be shy about.

9
00:00:36.224 --> 00:00:38.752
Kotlin and Java both run on the JVM.

10
00:00:38.752 --> 00:00:41.351
They compile to the same class files.

11
00:00:41.351 --> 00:00:44.723
So why can't Java just call my Kotlin, as it is?

12
00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:46.953
Here's the catch.

13
00:00:46.953 --> 00:00:50.750
javac, the Java compiler, never reads our Kotlin source.

14
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:56.039
It reads the class files that kotlinc wrote, and it reads them very literally.

15
00:00:56.933 --> 00:00:57.979
Let's map that.

16
00:00:57.979 --> 00:01:06.974
The house shorthand is everything that lives only in Kotlin source: reified functions, default arguments, objects, value classes.

17
00:01:06.974 --> 00:01:09.415
The printed form is the class file.

18
00:01:09.415 --> 00:01:13.877
And the partner driver, who only reads what's printed, is javac.

19
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:23.231
The lesson's demo has a small Kotlin API, written the way Kotlin code is usually written, and a Java class that calls it.

20
00:01:23.231 --> 00:01:27.272
Here are four ordinary lines a Java developer might write.

21
00:01:27.272 --> 00:01:29.153
From Kotlin, all four work.

22
00:01:29.153 --> 00:01:31.104
From Java, how many compile?

23
00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:35.655
None of them.

24
00:01:35.655 --> 00:01:39.075
Let's read javac's complaints, one at a time.

25
00:01:39.267 --> 00:01:43.104
First: no suitable constructor found for PlainBus(String).

26
00:01:43.104 --> 00:01:51.043
The Kotlin class has default arguments, and Java got only two constructors: the one with no arguments, and the full one.

27
00:01:51.043 --> 00:01:52.300
Nothing in between.

28
00:01:52.467 --> 00:01:55.874
Second: no suitable method found for subscribe.

29
00:01:55.874 --> 00:01:57.976
That's the reified subscribe.

30
00:01:57.976 --> 00:02:04.354
A reified function is pasted into each Kotlin caller, and javac can't paste Kotlin code.

31
00:02:04.354 --> 00:02:10.371
So kotlinc marks it synthetic, made by the compiler, and javac walks right past it.

32
00:02:10.533 --> 00:02:14.542
Third: cannot find symbol, constructor ParcelId(String).

33
00:02:14.542 --> 00:02:17.907
A value class's constructor is private to Java.

34
00:02:18.067 --> 00:02:21.878
Fourth: cannot find symbol, method describe(String).

35
00:02:21.878 --> 00:02:29.720
describe takes a value class, so its name in the class file is mangled: describe, a dash, and some letters.

36
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:35.217
The weight getter on an event comes out the same way, as getWeight-RqKWbHU.

37
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.008
That's a name only a compiler could love.

38
00:02:38.008 --> 00:02:41.189
Nobody on the support team should have to type it.

39
00:02:41.189 --> 00:02:45.196
So the point is: Java sees the printed form, not the shorthand.

40
00:02:45.196 --> 00:02:48.504
If we want Java at our door, we print what it needs.

41
00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:54.006
Each fix asks kotlinc to print one small thing on the form.

42
00:02:54.006 --> 00:02:59.002
JvmOverloads asks for one Java constructor per default argument.

43
00:02:59.002 --> 00:03:05.638
A second subscribe that takes Java's Class, an overload, is something javac can call.

44
00:03:05.638 --> 00:03:10.010
JvmStatic makes an object's member a real static method.

45
00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:19.300
JvmField exposes a constant as a plain field, and file JvmName names the class that holds a file's top-level functions.

46
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.273
With those fixes, the demo's Java side runs.

47
00:03:23.273 --> 00:03:25.927
The Class overload delivers the event.

48
00:03:25.927 --> 00:03:29.490
The KClass version still works, the long way round.

49
00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:33.261
And FriendlyBus, with JvmOverloads, takes just a name.

50
00:03:34.133 --> 00:03:37.919
That leaves the value classes, and here's the easy trap.

51
00:03:37.919 --> 00:03:43.192
The quick fix gives Java a way to pass a raw string or a raw long straight in.

52
00:03:43.192 --> 00:03:52.927
The value classes lesson warned about exactly this: JvmName on a function that takes a value class lets Java pass a raw long straight past init.

53
00:03:53.067 --> 00:03:56.752
In our picture, that's a side door with no clerk.

54
00:03:56.752 --> 00:03:58.632
It makes Java happy fast.

55
00:03:58.632 --> 00:04:04.649
It also lets in a parcel numbered 7781, which the front door would never accept.

56
00:04:05.533 --> 00:04:07.237
So we put the clerk back.

57
00:04:07.237 --> 00:04:15.208
A facade, the hotel reception desk from Adapter and Facade, is one small object that gives outsiders a simple way in.

58
00:04:15.208 --> 00:04:20.795
Ours takes plain values, a string and a long, and builds the value classes itself.

59
00:04:20.795 --> 00:04:22.499
So every init block runs.

60
00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:29.160
In Kotlin, Events.collected builds a ParcelId and a Weight, and hands back the event.

61
00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:34.784
Java calls it with a string and a number, and reads the grams back as a plain long.

62
00:04:34.933 --> 00:04:37.261
Now Java tries the bad id.

63
00:04:37.261 --> 00:04:42.812
The demo prints: refused from Java: invalid parcel id: '7781'.

64
00:04:42.812 --> 00:04:47.647
The side door is easy, and it still checks everything.

65
00:04:48.533 --> 00:04:50.631
Our picture breaks in one place.

66
00:04:50.631 --> 00:04:54.760
A real side door often has its own, looser rules for strangers.

67
00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:56.399
Ours has none of its own.

68
00:04:56.399 --> 00:04:59.611
It's the same rules, printed on a different form.

69
00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.713
And some of the side door came free, from earlier choices.

70
00:05:03.713 --> 00:05:11.068
Handler in E shows up in Java as Handler of question mark super E, so one Java handler fits any subscription.

71
00:05:11.068 --> 00:05:14.644
The sealed model lets a Java switch skip the default.

72
00:05:14.644 --> 00:05:21.459
And because handlers are fun interfaces with void methods, no Java lambda ever returns Unit.INSTANCE.

73
00:05:22.333 --> 00:05:23.327
One guardrail.

74
00:05:23.327 --> 00:05:30.137
Kotlin 2.4 has an experimental JvmExposeBoxed that would give Java the value-class constructors.

75
00:05:30.137 --> 00:05:35.316
In a quick test, the getter on the event interface itself stayed mangled.

76
00:05:35.316 --> 00:05:38.011
So keep the facade until that changes.

77
00:05:38.011 --> 00:05:44.041
And search your own Kotlin for public reified functions: what does a Java caller see?

78
00:05:44.933 --> 00:05:47.544
So, why can't Java just call my Kotlin?

79
00:05:47.544 --> 00:05:51.225
Because Java reads the printed form, not the shorthand.

80
00:05:51.225 --> 00:05:54.772
Print what it needs: an overload, a static, a facade.

81
00:05:54.772 --> 00:05:57.248
Make the side door easy, never loose.

82
00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:58.181
Quick check.

83
00:05:58.181 --> 00:06:02.412
Registry gets a new Kotlin function, weigh, which takes a Weight.

84
00:06:02.412 --> 00:06:03.648
What does Java see?

85
00:06:03.648 --> 00:06:07.944
And how do you let Java weigh a parcel without skipping the check?

86
00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:12.962
A mangled name it can't call.

87
00:06:12.962 --> 00:06:18.979
Add a facade function that takes a plain long and builds the Weight itself, so its init still runs.
